WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.810
- [Narrator] This Bitcoin mining facility

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.010
in upstate New York,

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.650
home to thousands of
computers running nearly 24/7

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:09.140
is just five miles from Niagara Falls,

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.610
near one of the largest
sources of hydro electricity

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.030
in the US.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.660
At the facility, workers
are attempting to build

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.000
a more sustainable way
to mine for Bitcoin,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.690
a process that has
generated a lot of criticism

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.010
for the amount of energy it consumes.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.604
Recent data estimates that globally

00:00:24.604 --> 00:00:27.600
it consumes over a hundred terawatt hours

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.200
of electricity annually,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.326
or more than the country of Finland.

00:00:31.326 --> 00:00:34.250
In 2018, one study estimated

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.586
that in less than 30 years,

00:00:35.586 --> 00:00:38.050
emissions from Bitcoin mining alone

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.310
could push up global warming
above two degrees Celsius

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.910
and recent crackdowns on
cryptocurrencies in China

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.580
are putting an even bigger spotlight

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.920
on the environmental
impact of the industry.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.650
- You have this Bitcoin mining
sector that is very hungry

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.066
for innovation and
technological solutions.

00:00:54.066 --> 00:00:57.250
- Bitcoin miners around the
world are making changes

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.674
that they say will make the
process more sustainable,

00:00:59.674 --> 00:01:02.410
both environmentally and financially.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.970
Here's a look at how this
transformation is playing out.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.590
The facility in Niagara Falls

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.574
is owned by Bit Digital and BlockFusion,

00:01:09.574 --> 00:01:11.066
two crypto mining companies

00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:12.940
that are harnessing the power of water

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.330
to cut down on their operations emissions.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.510
- This place was founded as a coal factory

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.633
to power a tire company.

00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:25.800
And this was decommissioned and repurposed

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.080
into a Bitcoin mining facility,
not using fossil fuels,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.600
but instead using Niagara Falls

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.650
and using that clean source of
power to power this facility.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.736
- Sam Tabar helps run Bit Digital,

00:01:37.736 --> 00:01:39.659
a member of the Crypto Climate Accord,

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:42.860
which aims to transition
the crypto industry

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.130
to net zero greenhouse
gas emissions by 2040.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.050
- We intend to be a
hundred percent renewable

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.150
way before then.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.940
We're about half renewables now,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.110
and in the next few months,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.210
we'll be dramatically
increasing that percentage.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.097
- According to Tabar,

00:01:55.097 --> 00:01:58.565
this facility which runs
largely on renewable hydro-power

00:01:58.565 --> 00:02:01.280
is almost entirely carbon neutral.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.630
The process starts here

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.597
at the Robert Moses Niagara Power Plant,

00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:08.500
where water from the Niagara
River run through 13 turbines

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.830
to produce renewable electricity.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.130
This is then transported

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.910
through high voltage transmission lines

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.640
to Bit Digital's facility,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.067
where it powers a warehouse
of mining machines.

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.700
The computers use Bitcoin's
proof of work method,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.875
which burns huge amounts of energy.

00:02:23.875 --> 00:02:26.290
This process is part of
how the cryptocurrency

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:27.466
builds its blockchain,

00:02:27.466 --> 00:02:30.667
which is key to keeping it
decentralized and secure.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:34.480
Around the world powerful
computers work simultaneously

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.298
to guess a complex sequence of numbers

00:02:36.298 --> 00:02:39.070
to validate a block of transactions.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.030
The miner whose computer gets there first,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.370
is awarded Bitcoin.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.727
This transaction is recorded
on the blockchain ledger,

00:02:44.727 --> 00:02:47.820
which is essentially
a digital record book.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:48.820
While secure,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.530
proof of work needs large
amounts of processing power

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.210
simply because it requires
all of the mining computers

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.810
to work on the same puzzle.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.560
- These math problems are very complicated

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.821
and they'll only increase in difficulty

00:03:00.821 --> 00:03:04.248
as more and more miners
come into the system.

00:03:04.248 --> 00:03:08.030
So there's no debate that it
takes a lot of electricity.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:13.030
The debate is where that source
of electricity comes from.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.969
- The process to unlock new
Bitcoins also has limits.

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:17.010
Right now,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.498
over 18 and a half million
Bitcoins have been mined

00:03:19.498 --> 00:03:22.660
out of a maximum 21 million.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.239
And the more miners that seek
out those remaining coins,

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:28.400
the more energy intensive
the process becomes.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.570
This is what is driving concern
over electricity consumption

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.090
in the crypto industry.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.670
- It is true that it is energy intensive.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.460
However, it's all about
the sources of energy.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.730
If you use clean sources of
energy, it's not a problem.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.780
If you're using dirty
sources, that's a problem.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.480
- While renewable sources of energy

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.611
release less CO2 into the
atmosphere than fossil fuels,

00:03:49.611 --> 00:03:51.990
experts say that hydropower facilities

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.570
can still have harmful impacts

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.590
on ecosystems and biodiversity.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.750
Bitcoin mining, both
renewable and non-renewable,

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.292
also generates electronic waste

00:04:00.292 --> 00:04:03.691
an estimated 11.5 kilotons per year.

00:04:03.691 --> 00:04:06.570
Bit Digital also runs a
number of other operations

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:07.810
that use renewables,

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.750
such as this solar powered
mining site in Nebraska.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.970
Other Bitcoin miners in the
US are eyeing nuclear power

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.360
and countries like Iceland
are home to operations

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.080
using geothermal energy.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:18.913
Tabar says,

00:04:18.913 --> 00:04:21.770
that along with his company's
transition to renewables,

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:24.920
Bit Digital is ramping up
operations in North America,

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:25.880
partly a reaction

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.540
to their recent regulatory
crackdowns in China.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.311
For years, the company, like
many other crypto miners,

00:04:31.311 --> 00:04:33.720
ran part of its business out of China.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:34.809
But just months ago,

00:04:34.809 --> 00:04:37.270
the country effectively
banned Bitcoin mining,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.410
which has driven many in the industry

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.660
to rapidly move operations
to other countries

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.080
like the US, Canada and Kazakhstan.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.100
- It's been a great thing
for the United States

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.920
because all this innovation
in the power sector

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.830
is happening as a result of this ban.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:55.090
The focus on finding
renewable sources of power

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.710
and creating technological solutions

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.320
to create further efficiencies.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.250
- For Tabard,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.220
transitioning the crypto
industry to renewable energy,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.758
isn't just for the good
of the environment.

00:05:03.758 --> 00:05:05.870
It also has financial benefits.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:06.919
- I don't think investors

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.719
are keen to invest in
Bitcoin mining companies

00:05:10.719 --> 00:05:14.077
that are not looking at
cleaner sources of power.

00:05:14.077 --> 00:05:17.730
That is not the right investment to make.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:18.563
We want to be profitable,

00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:21.100
but we don't want to destroy
the earth while we're at it.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.690
- While critics remain skeptical

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.688
of the crypto industries' energy demands,

00:05:24.688 --> 00:05:27.340
the push for a more
sustainable mining future

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.113
continues to pick up steam.

